2026-09-27 — musl daemon-failure stdout filesystem boundary (B08d6)

RED: retained original daemon-failure binaries linked separately against the
reference and 1,271-object mixed archives, unchanged reference runtest, unchanged
five-second watchdog. Two runs per archive/destination:
  stdout pipe:             4/4 exit 0
  guest-local regular file:4/4 exit 0
  host-mounted file:       4/4 watchdog FAIL [timed out], about 5.02--5.06 s
The passing observations sample completion at 0.25-second intervals; they are
not precise performance measurements. Previously isolated pipe runs did not
reproduce the actual Make recipe's `> case.err` on the virtiofs mount.

Small C reduction: fork, duplicate stdout until EMFILE, report that boundary,
then _Exit. No daemon, chdir, libc replacement or changed RLIMIT_NOFILE.
Both destinations reach 1,048,573 duplicates and EMFILE before exit. Guest-local
stdout passes; mounted stdout times out. The remaining child is in syscall 94
(AArch64 exit_group), repeatedly observed in request_wait_answer, not dup or
daemon. Kernel stack access was denied; no stack trace is claimed. Container
kernel is Linux 6.18.15, not the separately compiled EvoCC test kernel.

This is a reproducible execution-environment cause of these two timeouts, not
an inferred compiler arithmetic defect. The local Linux 6.18.44 source shows
close_files -> filp_close -> f_op->flush per descriptor and a FUSE_FLUSH request
path. That is corroborative source analysis, not an exact kernel/virtiofs trace
or a claim that a particular host-runtime implementation bug has been fixed.

Repair in the existing musl_case adapter:
  * For exactly the two daemon case paths, and only at the outer supervisor
    invocation, use an unlinked guest-/tmp regular stdout file.
  * Keep the original supervisor argv, five-second deadline, 100 KiB child stack,
    inner exec path, descriptor limit, library, source and assertions unchanged.
  * Copy output as exact bytes afterward; retain failure status, stderr and
    terminating signals. Setup/output errors fail rather than produce PASS.
  * Do not spool other tests or silently enlarge watchdogs. Record wrapper hash
    and the output policy in the next musl receipt for both compiler lanes.

GREEN: the reference/mixed retained original relinks, under both exact variant
paths, pass three repetitions each: 12 executions, about 0.19--0.23 seconds.
Those variant-path repetitions reuse each archive's one retained static relink;
they are not twelve independent builds. The two actual retained libc-test
executables additionally pass without relinking or changed hashes, at about
0.19/0.20 seconds. A deliberate twenty-second child still returns watchdog
failure after five seconds through the adapter; no timeout is converted to PASS.
Ten wrapper tests pass on macOS and sealed Linux: binary output (NUL/FF), exact
selection, setup failure, original argv, nonzero exit, signal and unaffected
cases. All 296 ladder Python tests and targeted Ruff/diff checks pass.

No full libc-test campaign receipt is replaced; historical 17 failures remain
historical. This closes the daemon environment subcase, not B08d or full musl.

